# Reusable Component Patterns

**When to apply**: When creating a component intended to be consumed like a native element -- design system primitives, shared selects, styled wrappers, etc. The AI should recognize this intent based on context.

**Does NOT apply to**: Page/route components, providers, context wrappers, or feature-specific components not meant to be reused as primitives.

## Design Philosophy

Reusable components should feel like native elements: accept `ref`, spread all non-specific props onto the root element, and allow full consumer customization. If a consumer can't override it, it's over-encapsulated.

## Ref Forwarding

> **React version**: The pattern below applies to **React 19+**, where `ref` is a regular prop.
> For **React 18**, use `forwardRef` (shown in the alternative below).

### React 19 — ref as a plain prop (preferred for React 19+ projects)

No `forwardRef`. Pass `ref` as a regular prop. Omit `'ref'` from the extended base type, then re-declare it.

Always use `React.ComponentRef<typeof Component>` to infer the ref element type from the wrapped component — never hardcode a DOM type like `HTMLDivElement`.

```tsx
// ✅ React 19 — ref as prop (replace BaseButtonProps / BaseButton with your library's types)
export type ButtonProps = Omit<BaseButtonProps, 'ref'> & {
  ref?: React.Ref<React.ComponentRef<typeof BaseButton>>
}

export function Button({ ref, ...props }: ButtonProps) {
  return <BaseButton ref={ref} {...props} />
}
```

### React 18 — forwardRef (use when targeting React 18)

```tsx
// ✅ React 18 — use forwardRef
export const Button = forwardRef<HTMLButtonElement, ButtonProps>((props, ref) => {
  return <BaseButton ref={ref} {...props} />
})
Button.displayName = 'Button'
```

```tsx
// ❌ Bad on React 19 — legacy pattern not needed when ref is a plain prop
export const Button = forwardRef<HTMLButtonElement, ButtonProps>((props, ref) => {
  return <BaseButton ref={ref} {...props} />
})
Button.displayName = 'Button'
```

## Props Pattern

Extend the underlying element/component props. Only destructure props that need component-specific logic -- spread everything else:

```tsx
// ✅ Good: only destructure what you transform
export function SearchInput({ onSearch, ref, ...inputProps }: SearchInputProps) {
  const handleChange = (e) => onSearch(e.target.value)
  return <input ref={ref} onChange={handleChange} {...inputProps} />
}

// ❌ Bad: destructuring props just to pass them through
export function SearchInput({ onSearch, disabled, placeholder, ref, ...rest }: SearchInputProps) {
  return <input ref={ref} disabled={disabled} placeholder={placeholder} {...rest} />
}
```

## Merging Default Slot/Style Props

> **Design system note**: This section shows an MUI-specific utility. Adapt it to your design system's equivalent.

### MUI — `mergeSlotProps`

When wrapping an MUI component, use `mergeSlotProps` so your defaults remain overridable by the consumer:

```tsx
import { mergeSlotProps, CardHeader as MuiCardHeader } from '@mui/material'

export function CardPageHeader(props: CardPageHeaderProps) {
  return (
    <MuiCardHeader
      {...props}
      slotProps={{
        ...props.slotProps,
        title: mergeSlotProps(props.slotProps?.title, {
          variant: 'body1',
          sx: { fontWeight: (theme) => theme.typography.fontWeightBold },
        }),
      }}
    />
  )
}
```

### Other design systems

| Design System | Equivalent pattern |
|---|---|
| Radix UI | Use the `asChild` prop to delegate rendering to the consumer's element |
| Chakra UI | Use `styleConfig` or spread `__css` defaults before consumer props |
| Plain HTML / CSS | Merge `className` strings (e.g. with `clsx`) and spread `style` after defaults |

The universal rule: **always place your default props before consumer props so consumers can override them.**

```tsx
// ✅ Generic — defaults first, consumer overrides second
export function Card({ className, style, ...props }: CardProps) {
  return (
    <div
      className={clsx('card-base', className)}
      style={{ borderRadius: 8, ...style }}
      {...props}
    />
  )
}
```

## File & Folder Structure

```
component-name/
├── index.ts          # Re-export only: export * from './component-name'
└── component-name.tsx
```

Register in the nearest parent barrel `index.ts`:

```ts
// index.ts
export * from './component-name'
```

## Exports

Use named exports. Export the component and its props type together at the bottom:

```tsx
export { ComponentName, type ComponentNameProps }
// or equivalently:
export function ComponentName(...) { ... }
export type { ComponentNameProps }
```
